  "query_preview": "..\n\nConvection was targeting Southeast Michigan from several angles this \nafternoon. The complex originating from the western UP/Wisconsin \nspilled into southwest Lower, merging with a line of storms that \nformed rapidly/linearly near the western shores of Lake Michigan. \nNear-term confidence is rising that all these storm interactions \nwill miss the local area, based on current trajectories. Regional \nradar trends alone continue to support avoidance of the most \nimpactful convection, with the strongest activity holding just west \nof the CWA. This keeps the most robust updrafts over the GRR and IWX \ndomains, reflecting an overall diminishing pattern for thunder as \nadvertised in the latest Hi-Res runs given a tendency for upstream \nconvection to steer southerly.\n\nFrom an environmental perspective, the synoptic drivers are absent \nthe locations of ongoing convection. The cold front(s) remain \ndisplaced well off to the north and west while weak capping persists \nlocally. 27.18Z KDTX RAOB indicates significant cap erosion compared \nto the prior measurement, but still not a state of free-convection \nwhich explains the lack of convection. Surface dewpoints are quite a \nbit lower than those upstream where storms fed off values in the \nupper 70s to mid 80s. Add to that ample cold air aloft and an \nuncapped environment. Note that 27.12Z ML products have aggressively \nbacked off severe threats, taking into account some basis of muted \nresolved diurnal convection. Some CAMs try again to get another \nround of organized storms through tonight (see 18Z HRRR), therefore \ncannot rule out potential through the early overnight hours. \nConditional severe threat remains, should storms finally \nmaterialize, but would favor elevated storms given the distribution \nof instability aloft. Based on the abysmal performance of NWP \nhandling local convection during the last 30 hours, latest PoP \nadjustments are a low confidence 35 percent Chance between now and \n06Z. Baseline blended guidance of 90 percent were too unreasonable. \nSatellite trends currently show a subsidence wake/dry slot moving in \nfrom northern Michigan, limiting potential during the evening time-\nframe.\n\nA mostly dry start is expected Tuesday morning as the jet streak \naloft crosses Lower Michigan. Geopotential heights initially hold \nsteady before gradually rising during the afternoon hours. Embedded \nweak vorticity signature should be inconsequential given the dryness \nof the column and warming mid-level profiles. 850 mb temperatures \nwill trend inversely, cooling into the low teens. High temperatures \nshould be capped in the upper 70s to lower 80s as flow turns \nnortherly during the day, modified by Lake Huron water temperatures \nin the 60s. Lower column anticyclone emerges over the Upper Midwest \nTuesday night with subsidence building overhead. This should make \nfor clearer skies and a seasonably cool night as dewpoints fall into \nthe 50s.\n\nHigh pressure tendency becomes established over portions of the \nGreat Lakes through midweek favoring a mainly dry forecast and \nbreezy afternoon periods. Temperatures gradually rise toward climo \nby the end of the week as surface flow flips southerly. Next \nopportunity for organized precipitation comes during the weekend.",
